<section class="section wrap" id="approach" aria-labelledby="approach-title">
  <div class="principles">
    <div class="principles__intro">
      <span class="mono" id="approach-title" data-reveal>How I direct</span>
      <h2 class="display-m" style="margin-top:18px" data-split>Deep systems, fair economies, and teams that ship.</h2>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:24px; max-width: 30em;" data-reveal>Four things I believe after six years of leading live games, and fifteen of competing in them.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="principles__list">
      <li class="principle" data-reveal>
        <span class="principle__num">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="principle__title">The first fifteen minutes decide the next fifteen months.</h3>
          <p class="principle__body">Onboarding is not a tutorial. It is the promise the rest of the game has to keep. At Anomaly I designed onboarding systems around the first quarter-hour of play, because every retention curve I have ever shipped was already written there.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="principle" data-reveal>
        <span class="principle__num">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="principle__title">Simulate before you ship.</h3>
          <p class="principle__body">I built MCTS- and CFR-inspired frameworks to pressure-test balance and player behaviour before a change reached a real player, and had AI agents play SHIPLESS 1,800 times before launch.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="principle" data-reveal>
        <span class="principle__num">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="principle__title">Ownership has to feel earned.</h3>
          <p class="principle__body">An economy is a promise about effort. Whether it is a ranked ladder, a battle pass, or a card someone truly owns, power should be earned in play, not just bought.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="principle" data-reveal>
        <span class="principle__num">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="principle__title">Hand people the keys.</h3>
          <p class="principle__body">The numbers I am proudest of are quieter than a retention chart: seven junior designers who became better designers. Direction means giving away work I could have kept, and giving feedback that makes someone sharper instead of smaller.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section wrap" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
